CALIBRATED OCCLUSION CASCADE 2. Execute all ten rounds on the initial object. Six labeled calibration beacons A..F stay fixed in world coordinates and are NOT object blocks. In every round recover a camera frame from its beacon panel: look L and up U are perpendicular signed world unit axes; screen right R=L cross U. A point p projects to (u,v)=(dot(p-center,R)+3,dot(p-center,U)+3). The camera is on the negative-L side, so smaller dot(p,L) is nearer. The mask has 0 for no shot; a positive k selects the k-th occupied cell along that ray (nearest=1). If fewer than k cells exist, it selects nothing. Select all hits H from the SAME pre-round object. Remove H. Reflect each hit about the plane through center perpendicular to R for reflect=u, U for reflect=v, or both for uv. TOGGLE those reflected cells: occupied becomes empty, empty becomes occupied. Then rotate the WHOLE resulting object by turns positive right-hand quarter turns about the specified camera axis through center. For a signed unit axis a, one positive quarter turn maps relative v to a cross v + a*dot(a,v). Camera calibration beacons never move. Subsequent rounds see the changed object. Return the complete final occupancy only if it meets the edit budget. Diagram rows increase upward and columns rightward; indices are printed. Occupancy panels use dark=filled, white=empty. Count panels print each integer and that many small pips below it; a gray ? is an unobserved count, not zero. Marker panels label colored cells A,B,C,D,E,F. The public diagram rectangles only describe layout, not cell values. 
